{"task_id": "Mbpp/123", "solution": "import math\ndef sum_proper_divisors(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of proper divisors for a given number n.\n    Proper divisors are positive divisors excluding n itself.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n\n    divisor_sum = 1  # 1 is always a proper divisor for n > 1\n    # Iterate from 2 up to the square root of n for efficiency.\n    for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            divisor_sum += i\n            # If i*i != n, then n // i is another distinct proper divisor.\n            if i * i != n:\n                divisor_sum += n // i\n    return divisor_sum\ndef amicable_numbers_sum(limit: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of all amicable numbers up to the specified limit.\n    An amicable pair (a, b) satisfies d(a)=b, d(b)=a, and a != b.\"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    # A set tracks numbers already identified as amicable to prevent double-counting.\n    found_amicable = set()\n\n    for n in range(1, limit + 1):\n        # Skip if n has already been added to total_sum as part of an amicable pair.\n        if n in found_amicable:\n            continue\n\n        d_n = sum_proper_divisors(n)\n\n        # Check if d_n is a potential candidate for an amicable pair:\n        # 1. d_n must be greater than n (to avoid perfect numbers and redundant checks).\n        # 2. d_n must be within the specified limit.\n        if d_n > n and d_n <= limit:\n            d_d_n = sum_proper_divisors(d_n)\n\n            # If d(d_n) equals n, then (n, d_n) form an amicable pair.\n            if d_d_n == n:\n                total_sum += n\n                total_sum += d_n\n                # Add both numbers of the pair to the set to mark them as found.\n                found_amicable.add(n)\n                found_amicable.add(d_n)\n\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/129", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef magic_square_test(matrix: List[List[int]]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if a given square matrix is a magic square.\n    It validates for empty or non-square matrices and compares sums of rows, columns, and both diagonals.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(matrix)\n\n    # Validate for empty matrix or matrix with empty rows\n    if n == 0 or (n > 0 and len(matrix[0]) == 0):\n        return False\n\n    # Validate for non-square matrix\n    for row in matrix:\n        if len(row) != n:\n            return False\n\n    # Calculate the target sum using the first row\n    target_sum = sum(matrix[0])\n\n    # Check all row sums\n    for row in matrix:\n        if sum(row) != target_sum:\n            return False\n\n    # Check all column sums\n    for j in range(n):\n        current_col_sum = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            current_col_sum += matrix[i][j]\n        if current_col_sum != target_sum:\n            return False\n\n    # Check main diagonal sum\n    main_diag_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        main_diag_sum += matrix[i][i]\n    if main_diag_sum != target_sum:\n        return False\n\n    # Check anti-diagonal sum\n    anti_diag_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        anti_diag_sum += matrix[i][n - 1 - i]\n    if anti_diag_sum != target_sum:\n        return False\n\n    # If all checks pass, it is a magic square\n    return True"}
