{"task_id": "Mbpp/255", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple, Any\ndef combinations_colors(input_list: List[Any], n: int) -> List[Tuple[Any, ...]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all combinations with repetition of elements from input_list,\n    where each combination has a length of n.\n\n    Args:\n        input_list: A list of elements of any data type.\n        n: An integer representing the desired length of combinations.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of tuples, where each tuple represents a unique combination.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        # As per the design document's function signature, negative n is handled with an error.\n        # However, the user's initial problem statement (assert combinations_colors(...) ) does not specify error handling,\n        # and the design implementation for generate_combinations includes a ValueError.\n        # For simplicity and to match common combination libraries behavior,\n        # we will handle n=0 to return [()] and for negative n, it should ideally raise an error or return [()] if it means empty combinations.\n        # Given the example only has n=1, and not explicit instructions on negative n from the user,\n        # adhering to the design's ValueError for n < 0 seems appropriate.\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a non-negative integer.\")\n    if n == 0:\n        return [()]\n    if not input_list:\n        # If n > 0 and the input_list is empty, no combinations can be formed.\n        return []\n\n    result = []\n    # Recursive helper function for combinations with repetition\n    def backtrack(current_combination, start_index):\n        if len(current_combination) == n:\n            result.append(tuple(current_combination))\n            return\n\n        for i in range(start_index, len(input_list)):\n            current_combination.append(input_list[i])\n            backtrack(current_combination, i) # 'i' for repetition\n            current_combination.pop()\n\n    backtrack([], 0)\n    return result"}
