{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the leftmost unset bit in an integer n to 1.\n    If all existing bits are set, a new most significant bit is added.\"\"\"\n\n    num_bits = n.bit_length()\n\n    # Iterate from the most significant bit down to the least significant bit\n    for i in range(num_bits - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the i-th bit is unset (0)\n        if ((n >> i) & 1) == 0:\n            # If unset, set it to 1 and return the new integer\n            return n | (1 << i)\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means all bits from 0 to num_bits - 1 are set.\n    # In this case, set the bit at position num_bits.\n    return n | (1 << num_bits)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the left-most unset bit (0) in the given integer to 1.\n    If all relevant bits are already set for the number's magnitude, the original integer is returned.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n >= 0:\n        if n == 0:\n            return 1\n\n        # For positive numbers, find the most significant bit that is 0 within its bit_length.\n        msb_pos = n.bit_length() - 1\n        for i in range(msb_pos, -1, -1):\n            if not (n & (1 << i)):  # If the i-th bit is 0\n                return n | (1 << i)\n\n        # If all bits up to msb_pos are 1, return the original number as per specification.\n        return n\n    else:  # n < 0\n        if n == -1: # For -1, all bits are effectively 1, so return original.\n            return n\n\n        # For negative numbers, find the right-most (least significant) 0-bit.\n        i = 0\n        while True:\n            if not (n & (1 << i)): # If the i-th bit is 0\n                return n | (1 << i)\n            i += 1"}
