{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(arr: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the pair of integers from an array that yields the highest product.\n    This function considers products of the two largest and two smallest numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple containing the pair of integers with the highest product, sorted in ascending order.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input array contains fewer than two integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(arr) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array must contain at least two integers.\")\n\n    arr_sorted = sorted(arr)\n\n    # The two largest numbers are at the end of the sorted array.\n    max1 = arr_sorted[-1]\n    max2 = arr_sorted[-2]\n\n    # The two smallest numbers are at the beginning of the sorted array.\n    min1 = arr_sorted[0]\n    min2 = arr_sorted[1]\n\n    # Calculate the products of these candidate pairs.\n    product_of_largest = max1 * max2\n    product_of_smallest = min1 * min2\n\n    # Return the pair that yields the highest product, ensuring the output tuple is sorted.\n    if product_of_largest >= product_of_smallest:\n        # Return the pair of largest numbers, sorted\n        return (min(max1, max2), max(max1, max2))\n    else:\n        # Return the pair of smallest numbers, sorted\n        return (min(min1, min2), max(min1, min2))"}
