{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def _normalize_tuple(t: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Sorts elements within a tuple to create a canonical representation for order-agnostic comparison.\"\"\"\n    return tuple(sorted(t))\ndef tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the intersection of two lists of tuples, treating tuples as unordered.\n    Returns a set of unique normalized tuples found in both input lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    normalized_set1 = set()\n    for t in list1:\n        normalized_set1.add(_normalize_tuple(t))\n\n    normalized_set2 = set()\n    for t in list2:\n        normalized_set2.add(_normalize_tuple(t))\n\n    return normalized_set1.intersection(normalized_set2)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def tuple_intersection(list1: list[tuple], list2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the set intersection of two lists of tuples, ignoring element order.\"\"\"\n    # Normalize tuples in both lists by sorting their internal elements.\n    normalized_list1 = [tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list1]\n    normalized_list2 = [tuple(sorted(t)) for t in list2]\n\n    # Convert normalized lists to sets and find their intersection.\n    set1 = set(normalized_list1)\n    set2 = set(normalized_list2)\n\n    return set1.intersection(set2)"}
