{"task_id": "Mbpp/743", "solution": "def rotate_right(input_list: list, rotation_count: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates a list to the right by a specified number of items.\n    Returns a new list with elements shifted rightwards.\n    \"\"\"\n    list_length = len(input_list)\n\n    if list_length == 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Calculate the effective rotation count, handling rotations larger than list length or negative rotations.\n    effective_rotation_count = rotation_count % list_length\n\n    # If effective_rotation_count is 0, the list is effectively unchanged; return a shallow copy.\n    if effective_rotation_count == 0:\n        return input_list[:]\n\n    # Split the list into two parts and concatenate them to form the rotated list.\n    # The last 'effective_rotation_count' elements move to the beginning.\n    rotated_list = input_list[-effective_rotation_count:] + input_list[:-effective_rotation_count]\n\n    return rotated_list"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/743", "solution": "def rotate_right(list_to_rotate, n):\n    \"\"\"Rotates a list to the right by n positions.\n    Handles empty lists and calculates effective rotation for various n values.\n    \"\"\"\n    list_length = len(list_to_rotate)\n\n    if list_length == 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Calculate the effective number of positions to rotate to the right.\n    # Python's modulo operator correctly handles positive and negative n.\n    effective_n = n % list_length\n\n    # Perform the rotation by slicing and concatenating the list.\n    # The last 'effective_n' elements are moved to the beginning.\n    rotated_list = list_to_rotate[-effective_n:] + list_to_rotate[:-effective_n]\n\n    return rotated_list"}
